1 | devops | Branch feat/ac-host-carries-intent. Amend docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md section 3.3 per the sign-off on issue 2340: on refine (3.3.3), get_option (3.3.4) and update_trip (3.3.7) the search or trip identifier becomes optional, with the rule that when absent the connector uses the most recent search on that binding and the host names one only when several exist; revision is removed from every host-facing input; the concurrency guarantee is restated as the connector reading the current revision immediately before a write and answering revision_conflict with the current state if it moved; section 3.2 gains one sentence that identifiers and revisions are carried by the connector, never required from the host; section 3.4 gains the note that an intent_change refusal on refine carries a hint field naming the tool to call next (search) so the host can proceed without a human. Date the amendment, name the co-founder, cite the issue comment. VERIFY: make check-prd-index exit 0. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Commit and push. | feat/ac-host-carries-intent

2 | go-backend | On branch feat/ac-host-carries-intent after wave 1, in services/iris: implement the amended section 3.3. ONE, a per-binding current-search pointer, durable in the same Redis the spend meter uses (key by binding id, set on every served search, read when an identifier is absent); refine, get_option and update_trip accept a missing identifier and resolve it from the pointer, answering not_found with a hint when there is none. TWO, remove revision from the host-facing schemas and arg structs of refine and update_trip; Iris reads the record immediately before writing, sends that revision as expected_revision, and maps the API 409 to revision_conflict carrying the current state; keep the settle-on-proof logic from PR 2838 exactly. THREE, the intent_change refusal on refine carries hint next_tool search with the reason, and the manifest description of refine says a stay cannot be added by refine. FOUR, the manifest descriptions of the three tools state the new optional identifier in plain words a host will follow. Tests: identifier absent resolves to the latest search; two searches then a refine with no id targets the newest; a concurrent website edit between read and write yields revision_conflict with the current record; the schemas no longer contain revision. VERIFY: go test ./... with the race flag in services/iris, real exit codes in the PR body; update the PR body with what the host approval box now shows for a refine. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Open a draft PR with Refs 2800 and 2340. | feat/ac-host-carries-intent

3 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the PR on branch feat/ac-host-carries-intent. No commits, no push, no review, no labels; tmp writes only. Record head SHA at start and at report. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: does moving the revision into Iris weaken the concurrency guarantee. Race it: website write between the Iris read and write must produce revision_conflict with the current record, never a silent overwrite; two bindings for the same traveller (two hosts) must each resolve their own most recent search, never that of the other host; a binding with no search yet must get not_found with the hint; the intent_change refusal must carry the hint. Grep the host-facing manifest for revision and for any identifier marked required on the three tools; both must be gone. Check the PRD amendment names the co-founder and the date and that the schemas match it. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC HOST CARRIES INTENT with SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX and citations. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/ac-host-carries-intent

4 | security-reviewer | SECURITY REVIEW of the PR on branch feat/ac-host-carries-intent. Read-only; tmp writes only. Record head SHA at start and at report. The change adds a per-binding pointer to the most recent search in Redis and lets a tool call act without naming a record. Check: the pointer is keyed by binding, so a token for another binding can never resolve to the search of this traveller; a revoked binding cannot use its pointer; the pointer never carries a traveller name or anything beyond an identifier; a crafted identifier still returns not_found with no leak; the concurrency guard cannot be bypassed by omitting the identifier. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads SECURITY HOST CARRIES INTENT with a final SAFE or BLOCK line. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/ac-host-carries-intent
